-- Source module: Solutions.SynthLevels
/-!
# §6.1 Butterflies, decimation in frequency, and the stored frequency order

One level of the decimation-in-frequency procedure on an axis of length `t = 2^a`, at named slot
`h` (address bit `h`): the butterfly `H0` on bit `h`, then the twiddle `ω_{2^{h+1}}^{-b k}` where
`b` is the new branch bit and `k = x mod 2^h` is encoded by the unprocessed lower slots
(`lev`). After `s` levels (slots `a-1, …, a-s`) the array is the partial transform with kernel
`Ws s` (`levCore`): the processed slots hold the frequency bits `0, …, s-1` (from the most
significant slot), which is the inductive description of §6.1.

Multidimensional arrays `(Fin D → ℕ) → R`: `axisK i W` applies a one-dimensional kernel along
axis `i`, `axisL i h` a level along axis `i`. Levels on different axes commute with each other's
kernels, so *any* schedule that runs every axis's slots from high to low gives the same result
(`runF_sched`). With `rev` the bit reversal (`Bt`), the result is `(B F^-) z` (`synth_forward`):
the entry with frequency `j` is stored at `rev j`. The paper's round schedule (round `h` on all
axes with `a_i > h`, for `h = ℓ-1, …, 0`) is such a schedule (`paperSched_ok`).
-/

